        <h1>I Am Enough: Challenging the Stigma of Singleness</h1>
        <p class="hero-lede">What happens when well-meaning questions and assumptions make singleness feel like a flaw to be fixed?</p>
        <div class="article-meta">
          <span class="article-author"><img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dtrk4dyly/image/upload/v1741111725/Chelsey-min_c2pd5a.jpg" alt="Chelsey Pontius"><span>Written by <strong>Chelsey Pontius</strong></span></span>
          <span>February 4, 2023</span><span>8 minute read</span><span>Identity &amp; Relationships</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="wrap article-layout">
      <article class="article-body">
        <p class="lead">I came across an article one morning discussing “single shaming.” I had never heard the term before, but it named something I had felt for years. It validated my experience and gave me the push to finally say what I had been needing to say.</p>

        <h2>Single shaming: What is it?</h2>
        <p>I have often experienced single shaming from well-meaning people who care about me but do not understand how their questions or assumptions feel. Society and media frequently portray single people—especially single women—as flawed, incomplete, desperate, lonely, needy, or insecure.</p>
        <p>The focus on relationship status begins early. Adults ask children whether a friend or classmate is their “boyfriend” or “girlfriend” before they understand what those words mean. Later, social gatherings can become uncomfortable when the first topic is a person’s lack of a romantic relationship, followed by awkward comments or advice that was never requested.</p>
        <p>Single shaming is a negative bias toward people who are not partnered. It assumes they must be sad, searching, or somehow responsible for ending up alone. Even familiar phrases such as “your missing piece” or “your other half” can imply that a person is incomplete without a romantic partner.</p>
        <p>The original essay cited reporting that 52% of 1,000 adults surveyed in the United Kingdom had experienced single shaming since the start of the pandemic. It also noted a broader cultural shift: many adults now spend more of their lives single, even while our attitudes have been slower to change.</p>

        <h2>My stand</h2>
        <p>After finally finding a name for the frustration and inadequacy I had felt, I was relieved—but also indignant. Questions about my lack of a partner often arrived before questions about any other part of my life, my feelings, or my growth.</p>
        <blockquote class="pullquote">“I am enough and more than okay being single right now.”</blockquote>
        <p>I am not against marriage or romantic relationships. Romance and marriage can be beautiful when they are healthy. The problem is the over-glamorized cultural idea that a relationship is something we need in order to be worthwhile. I refuse to rush into something or stay in something unhealthy simply because being single might be viewed as lonely or unwanted.</p>

        <h2>The frustration</h2>
        <p>It is difficult when a conversation cannot happen without someone asking about relationship status or offering sympathy. I have heard, “You’ll find someone someday,” and, “It’ll be your turn too.” I have been set up with people when I never asked for help. These comments can make it sound as if being single means being flawed, incomplete, sad, or lonely.</p>
        <p>I know most of these comments come from care, not cruelty. Still, impact matters. When someone repeatedly focuses on the absence of a relationship, it can unintentionally communicate that everything else about a person is less important.</p>

        <h2>Why I’m okay</h2>
        <p>Society has pushed a narrow picture of what is supposed to make us happy and complete. Over time, I have learned to trust my own growth and my relationship with God instead of following the world’s timetable. My faith is what defines me. God made me for a unique purpose and calling, and I am still that person—with or without a partner.</p>
        <p>I am not perfect, and there are parts of myself I still need to work on. A relationship will not suddenly fix those things or complete me. A future partner would still be a person with needs and challenges of their own.</p>
        <p>Singleness has given me room to know myself, deepen my faith, develop my gifts, make decisions thoughtfully, and invest in family and friends. I can celebrate other people’s healthy relationships without asking, “Why not me?” A romantic relationship can be a place of growth, but it is not the only place where growth happens.</p>

        <h2>How you can challenge the stereotype</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Start within.</strong> Examine what you believe about singleness. It is difficult to correct a stereotype in others if you still believe it yourself.</li>
          <li><strong>Get to know yourself.</strong> Learn what you want, what you need, what you value, and where you want to grow.</li>
          <li><strong>Let go of the idealized picture.</strong> Much of what surrounds us treats partnership as the only real path to happiness. Understand that message so you can question it, forgive people who repeat it unknowingly, and replace it with something more truthful.</li>
          <li><strong>Correct the comments.</strong> Respond in a way that fits the moment. A calm, private conversation is often best with family or repeat offenders. Explain that you understand their intentions while also naming how their words make you feel.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>In my own experience, a general social media post helped. I named and defined single shaming and explained that, although the intentions behind the comments were often good, the result was hurtful and frustrating. Once people understood where I was coming from, many of the comments stopped.</p>

        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p>I hope this reflection helps someone recognize the stereotype, relate to the frustration, or feel brave enough to speak up. If you have felt the sting of rejection, unwantedness, anxiety, or fear that you are incomplete without a partner, remember this: regardless of your relationship status or dating history, God loves you—and you are enough.</p>

